<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>Confirmation is Not "Graduation"</h4>
<p>In many places, Confirmation is seen as "the last class of catechesis," which is deeply mistaken. Confirmation is the beginning of a new mission, not the end. The confirmed do not "graduate"; they are sent as apostles. Watch your language: do not use the school metaphor.</p>

<h4>Baptism and Confirmation</h4>
<p>In the East, Confirmation is administered with Baptism (even to infants). In the West, the historical separation between Baptism and Confirmation has made many see Confirmation as something separate. Remember that both form one "Christian initiation." Confirmation "confirms" and strengthens what was received in Baptism.</p>

<h4>The Seven Gifts</h4>
<p>Do not teach the gifts as a school list to memorize. Each one responds to a human need: wisdom to see with God's eyes, understanding to grasp the deep, counsel for decisions, fortitude for difficulty, knowledge to discern, piety for devotion, fear of God for reverence. Help relate each gift to a concrete situation.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age-Specific Adaptations</h3>

<h4>🌱 Seed (children 7-12)</h4>
<p>Use Pentecost as the "birthday of the Church." Activities: color the seven gifts, write each gift on a heart. Teach "Come, Holy Spirit." Explain that Confirmation makes them "soldiers of Christ" (in a healthy sense: defenders of the faith).</p>

<h4>🌿 Sprouts (teens 13-17)</h4>
<p>Connection with their lives: Do they feel prepared to "defend the faith" in a secular world? What does being "confirmed" mean among peers? Ask: "When was the last time you had to defend your faith?" The seven gifts applied to school, friends, family situations.</p>

<h4>🪴 Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults come from Protestant traditions without Confirmation as sacrament. Help them understand that Confirmation is not an optional "growth sacrament," but part of complete Christian initiation. The bishop as minister shows the unity of the Church.</p>

<h4>🌳 Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Deepen the theology of "anointing" in the Old Testament (prophets, kings, priests). Confirmation configures us as priests, prophets, and kings by participation. May cite Saint Cyril of Jerusalem, Mystagogical Catecheses.</p>

</section>

